    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d133-a5">

                <pb facs="0281" n="225" sameAs="frus:frus1876/pg225"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 5 in No. 76.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Lord Tenterden to Mr. Hoffman</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Foreign Office</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1876-05-02"><hi rendition="#italic">May</hi> 2,
                        1876</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#flush"><hi rendition="#italic">Pressing</hi>.</p>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to my letter of yesterday, I
                    have the honor to inclose for your information a copy of a notice, just received
                    from the home office, which has been addressed to that department by Messrs.
                    Wontner &amp; Sons, solicitors, stating that an application will be made
                    to-morrow at twelve o’clock to a judge at chambers for the issue of a writ of
                        <hi rendition="#italic">habeas corpus</hi> in the case of E. D. Winslow.</p>
                <p>In forwarding this notice, the secretary of state for the home department has
                    informed me that he will endeavor to show cause why the prisoner should not be
                    set at liberty, but that he is unable to guarantee the result.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>TENTERDEN.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>In the absence of the Earl of Derby.</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
